    "Patients with diabetes and NSTE-ACS face higher in-hospital and long-term risks but undergo less frequent revascularization. Studies indicate that diabetic patients have worse risk profiles and outcomes. There is limited research focusing on diabetes-specific interventions in ACS. However, studies such as SYNTAX and FREEDOM provide insights into multivessel treatments for diabetic patients. The SYNTAX trial highlighted better outcomes for more complex cases with CABG. Antiplatelet therapy, including clopidogrel and ticagrelor, is recommended for patients with diabetes, as seen in trials such as TRITON-TIMI 38 and PLATO. Recommendations for post-CABG patients suggest continued use of antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapies.": "NSTE-ACS, ACS, PCI, CABG, FREEDOM, SYNTAX, TRITON-TIMI 38, PLATO, and other trials assessed interventions for these patients. Recommended treatments for post-CABG patients include antiplatelet and anticoagulant therapy to mitigate increased event risks."
